<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<manifest>
  <!--
    The version here is what the Power Platform upgrades on, and the release
    workflow refuses to publish a tag that disagrees with it. Bump this in the
    same commit as the tag.
  -->
  <control namespace="PCFHub"
           constructor="GridDataBars"
           version="0.2.0"
           display-name-key="GridDataBars_Name"
           description-key="GridDataBars_Desc"
           control-type="virtual">

    <external-service-usage enabled="false" />

    <!--
      The control's only property, and it is not a setting.

      A grid customizer is configured by being *named* on a grid, not by having
      values set on it. The Power Apps grid generates an event name, passes it
      in through this property, and listens on it; the control fires its cell
      renderers and editors back through `context.factory.fireEvent(eventName,
      …)`. Nothing else about this control is configurable, and an empty value
      here means it does nothing at all — the platform is what fills it in.

      Bound rather than input because that is what the template Microsoft
      publishes declares, and the grid binds it rather than passing a static
      string. It is also why index.ts fires from `updateView` as well as
      `init`: a bound property is not guaranteed to carry its value on the
      first pass.

      Do not add maker-facing input properties here expecting to configure the
      overrides. They would work, but a customizer is assigned to a table's
      whole grid, so any value set on it applies to every column of that type
      on every view of that table — which is almost never what the setting was
      meant to say. Narrow inside the override instead, off
      `colDefs[columnIndex].name`.

      KEEP THE NAME `EventName`. In the platform the host generates the value,
      but nothing does in PCFHub's demo harness, so the harness seeds it — and
      it finds the property by looking for one called `EventName`, falling back
      to the single text input a customizer manifest has. Rename this and give
      the control a second text property and the harness stops finding it: the
      demo loads cleanly, `init` returns at its own guard, and nothing on the
      page says why.
    -->
    <property name="EventName"
              display-name-key="EventName_Name"
              description-key="EventName_Desc"
              of-type="SingleLine.Text"
              usage="bound"
              required="true" />

    <resources>
      <code path="index.ts" order="1" />
      <css path="css/GridDataBars.css" order="1" />
      <!--
        React 16.14.0 and Fluent 8, matching the grid customizer template
        Microsoft publishes. Note the 8 — the rest of this repository's React
        variants are on Fluent 9, and this one is deliberately not.

        Not a nostalgic choice: the elements these overrides return are rendered
        by whichever React instance the *host* owns, so the hooks inside them
        have to dispatch through that same instance — which is what declaring
        them as platform libraries guarantees. Fluent 8 in particular needs no
        provider above it, and a cell renderer has nowhere to put one: the grid
        mounts each returned element on its own, inside a cell.
      -->
      <platform-library name="React" version="16.14.0" />
      <platform-library name="Fluent" version="8.121.1" />
      <resx path="strings/GridDataBars.1033.resx" version="1.0.0" />
    </resources>

    <!--
      No feature-usage, and that is a finding rather than an omission.

      A bar learns its range from the column's `MinValue`/`MaxValue`, and the
      obvious way to read those is `context.utils.getEntityMetadata`, gated
      behind `Utility`. That call does not carry them. Its attribute metadata
      exposes `AttributeType`, `DisplayName`, `EntityLogicalName` and
      `LogicalName` and nothing numeric — verified on a decimal column with both
      ends set in the table designer, where both read `undefined`. The range
      exists only on the typed Web API metadata entities, which the collection
      has to be *cast* to before the properties can be named at all.

      So the lookup is a same-origin `fetch` against `/api/data/v9.2/`, which
      needs no declared feature: `context.webAPI` is gated, but it addresses
      records by entity logical name and cannot reach `EntityDefinitions`.
      Declaring a feature this control does not call would be a lie in the one
      file an administrator reads to find out what it touches.

      Where the request cannot succeed — no Dataverse behind the page, signed
      out, a preview harness — every column misses its bounds, every renderer
      declines, and the grid draws its own cells. That is the same output as a
      table whose columns declare no ranges: inert rather than broken.
    -->
  </control>
</manifest>
